WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.635
NARRATOR: This lesson builds the derivative
from first principles.

2
00:00:03.635 --> 00:00:08.175
NARRATOR: We will begin with an average rate
of change over an interval, shrink that

3
00:00:08.175 --> 00:00:11.505
interval using a limit, and obtain the
derivative at one point.

4
00:00:11.505 --> 00:00:17.098
NARRATOR: Then we will turn that pointwise
idea into a derivative function, recognize

5
00:00:17.098 --> 00:00:22.261
where differentiability fails, interpret
motion, and use a tangent line for local

6
00:00:22.261 --> 00:00:22.691
approximation.

7
00:00:22.691 --> 00:00:26.364
NARRATOR: The goal is not merely to memorize f
prime notation.

8
00:00:26.364 --> 00:00:30.960
NARRATOR: It is to understand what information
a derivative contains, how the definition

9
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.788
produces it, and when that information is
valid.

10
00:00:33.788 --> 00:00:39.760
NARRATOR: Keep one question in view: how can
we describe change at an exact input when an

11
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.219
ordinary slope calculation needs two different
points?

12
00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:46.100
NARRATOR: Choose an input a and a second input
a plus h.

13
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.294
NARRATOR: The change in input is h.

14
00:00:48.294 --> 00:00:54.926
NARRATOR: The corresponding change in output
is f of a plus h minus f of a. Dividing output

15
00:00:54.926 --> 00:00:57.873
change by input change gives the difference
quotient.

16
00:00:57.873 --> 00:01:02.567
NARRATOR: Geometrically, it is the slope of
the secant line through the two points.

17
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:06.342
NARRATOR: In an application, it is an average
rate across the interval.

18
00:01:06.342 --> 00:01:11.648
NARRATOR: If position changes by one hundred
meters over five seconds, the average velocity

19
00:01:11.648 --> 00:01:17.332
is twenty meters per second, even if the
object moved faster or slower within those

20
00:01:17.332 --> 00:01:18.090
five seconds.

21
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.832
NARRATOR: Average change summarizes an
interval.

22
00:01:20.832 --> 00:01:24.595
NARRATOR: It does not yet isolate what happens
at a single instant.

23
00:01:24.722 --> 00:01:28.498
NARRATOR: To focus on the input a, let h
approach zero.

24
00:01:28.498 --> 00:01:34.365
NARRATOR: We do not substitute h equals zero
into the quotient; that would divide by zero.

25
00:01:34.365 --> 00:01:40.182
NARRATOR: Instead, we examine values of h that
are nonzero but arbitrarily small.

26
00:01:40.182 --> 00:01:45.896
NARRATOR: As the second point approaches the
first, the secant lines may approach a

27
00:01:45.896 --> 00:01:46.712
limiting line.

28
00:01:46.712 --> 00:01:52.315
NARRATOR: If their slopes approach one finite
value from both sides, that value is f prime

29
00:01:52.315 --> 00:01:54.416
of a, the derivative at a.

30
00:01:54.416 --> 00:01:57.465
NARRATOR: The limit is the crucial reasoning
step.

31
00:01:57.465 --> 00:02:03.115
NARRATOR: It converts a family of nearby
average rates into one instantaneous rate.

32
00:02:03.115 --> 00:02:08.401
NARRATOR: If the slopes do not settle to a
common finite value, the derivative at that

33
00:02:08.401 --> 00:02:09.723
point does not exist.

34
00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:12.609
NARRATOR: Let f of x equal x squared.

35
00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:19.790
NARRATOR: Start with f of a plus h minus f of
a, all divided by h. Expanding gives a squared

36
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:24.458
plus two a h plus h squared minus a squared,
divided by h.

37
00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:26.614
NARRATOR: The a-squared terms cancel.

38
00:02:26.614 --> 00:02:31.739
NARRATOR: Because h is nonzero during the
limiting process, factor h from the numerator

39
00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:33.935
and cancel it with the denominator.

40
00:02:33.935 --> 00:02:36.270
NARRATOR: The quotient becomes two a plus h.

41
00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.361
NARRATOR: Now let h approach zero.

42
00:02:38.361 --> 00:02:45.149
NARRATOR: The limit is two a. Notice the order
of operations: form the quotient, simplify it

43
00:02:45.149 --> 00:02:48.119
for nonzero h, then evaluate the limit.

44
00:02:48.119 --> 00:02:53.043
NARRATOR: We never claim that h itself is zero
while it remains in the denominator.

45
00:02:53.186 --> 00:02:58.429
NARRATOR: The previous calculation kept a
symbolic, so it did more than find one slope.

46
00:02:58.429 --> 00:03:04.896
NARRATOR: It produced a rule: for f of x
equals x squared, f prime of x equals two x.

47
00:03:04.896 --> 00:03:10.242
NARRATOR: This new function reports the
original curve's tangent slope at every

48
00:03:10.242 --> 00:03:11.133
differentiable input.

49
00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:14.730
NARRATOR: At x equals three, the derivative is
six.

50
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.518
NARRATOR: At x equals negative two, it is
negative four.

51
00:03:18.518 --> 00:03:20.776
NARRATOR: At zero, it is zero.

52
00:03:20.776 --> 00:03:24.296
NARRATOR: The derivative function has its own
graph and domain.

53
00:03:24.296 --> 00:03:29.108
NARRATOR: Its input is still an x-value from
the original problem, while its output is a

54
00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.010
rate or slope.

55
00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:35.982
NARRATOR: Thinking of f prime as a function is
essential for later topics such as

56
00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.574
optimization and differential equations.

57
00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.200
NARRATOR: The sign of the derivative
summarizes local direction.

58
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:47.430
NARRATOR: Where f prime is positive, tangent
lines rise from left to right, so the function

59
00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.598
is locally increasing.

60
00:03:48.598 --> 00:03:54.443
NARRATOR: Where f prime is negative, tangent
lines fall, so the function is locally

61
00:03:54.443 --> 00:03:54.861
decreasing.

62
00:03:54.861 --> 00:03:59.682
NARRATOR: Where f prime equals zero, the
tangent is horizontal.

63
00:03:59.682 --> 00:04:05.264
NARRATOR: A zero derivative identifies a
critical point candidate, not automatically a

64
00:04:05.264 --> 00:04:06.659
maximum or minimum.

65
00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:12.647
NARRATOR: For x squared, f prime changes from
negative to positive at zero, matching the

66
00:04:12.647 --> 00:04:13.445
parabola's minimum.

67
00:04:13.445 --> 00:04:18.228
NARRATOR: But another function can have a
horizontal tangent and continue increasing.

68
00:04:18.228 --> 00:04:23.693
NARRATOR: The derivative gives local evidence;
interpreting a larger pattern requires

69
00:04:23.693 --> 00:04:27.667
checking how that evidence changes around the
point.

70
00:04:27.794 --> 00:04:32.948
NARRATOR: Not every graph has an ordinary
finite tangent slope everywhere.

71
00:04:32.948 --> 00:04:39.010
NARRATOR: At a jump or hole, the function is
not continuous, so it cannot be differentiable

72
00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:39.389
there.

73
00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:44.580
NARRATOR: At a sharp corner, the left-hand and
right-hand slopes approach different values.

74
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:48.840
NARRATOR: At a cusp, the slopes can diverge
with incompatible signs.

75
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.434
NARRATOR: At a vertical tangent, the slope
becomes unbounded rather than approaching a

76
00:04:54.434 --> 00:04:55.295
finite number.

77
00:04:55.295 --> 00:05:00.218
NARRATOR: Differentiability is therefore a
stronger condition than continuity.

78
00:05:00.218 --> 00:05:04.618
NARRATOR: If a function is differentiable at a
point, it is continuous there.

79
00:05:04.618 --> 00:05:09.679
NARRATOR: The reverse is not guaranteed: the
absolute-value function is continuous at zero

80
00:05:09.679 --> 00:05:14.351
but has a corner, so its derivative at zero
does not exist.

81
00:05:14.498 --> 00:05:18.134
NARRATOR: Suppose s of t gives position along
a line.

82
00:05:18.134 --> 00:05:22.254
NARRATOR: A secant slope over a time interval
gives average velocity.

83
00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:26.960
NARRATOR: The derivative s prime of t gives
instantaneous velocity.

84
00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:32.078
NARRATOR: Its sign indicates direction
relative to the chosen coordinate axis, while

85
00:05:32.078 --> 00:05:33.784
its magnitude is speed.

86
00:05:33.784 --> 00:05:37.317
NARRATOR: Differentiate velocity to obtain
acceleration.

87
00:05:37.317 --> 00:05:42.494
NARRATOR: Positive acceleration does not
necessarily mean the object is speeding up;

88
00:05:42.494 --> 00:05:48.533
speed increases when velocity and acceleration
have the same sign and decreases when their

89
00:05:48.533 --> 00:05:49.396
signs differ.

90
00:05:49.396 --> 00:05:51.641
NARRATOR: Units also reveal meaning.

91
00:05:51.641 --> 00:05:57.814
NARRATOR: If position is measured in meters
and time in seconds, velocity uses meters per

92
00:05:57.814 --> 00:06:01.106
second and acceleration uses meters per second
squared.

93
00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:05.268
NARRATOR: The derivative is also the
coefficient of a local linear model.

94
00:06:05.268 --> 00:06:11.340
NARRATOR: At x equals a, the tangent line has
value f of a and slope f prime of a.

95
00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:18.189
NARRATOR: Its equation is L of x equals f of a
plus f prime of a times x minus a.

96
00:06:18.189 --> 00:06:22.756
NARRATOR: For x close to a, L of x can
approximate f of x.

97
00:06:22.756 --> 00:06:28.565
NARRATOR: For example, to estimate the square
root of four point one, use f of x equals

98
00:06:28.565 --> 00:06:30.957
square root x at a equals four.

99
00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:36.839
NARRATOR: The value is two and the derivative
is one fourth, so the estimate is two plus one

100
00:06:36.839 --> 00:06:40.434
fourth times zero point one, or two point zero
two five.

101
00:06:40.434 --> 00:06:45.862
NARRATOR: The method works locally; accuracy
generally decreases farther from the base

102
00:06:45.862 --> 00:06:46.314
point.

103
00:06:46.442 --> 00:06:53.665
NARRATOR: Let f of x equal three x squared
minus two x, and find the tangent at x equals

104
00:06:53.665 --> 00:06:54.045
one.

105
00:06:54.045 --> 00:06:59.504
NARRATOR: The derivative function is six x
minus two, so f prime of one is four.

106
00:06:59.504 --> 00:07:05.090
NARRATOR: The point on the original graph is
one comma f of one, which is one comma one.

107
00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:10.575
NARRATOR: Using point-slope form, y minus one
equals four times x minus one.

108
00:07:10.575 --> 00:07:15.872
NARRATOR: This example separates three objects
that students often mix together: f of one is

109
00:07:15.872 --> 00:07:22.581
the output one; f prime of one is the slope
four; and the tangent line is a new linear

110
00:07:22.581 --> 00:07:23.994
equation built from both.

111
00:07:23.994 --> 00:07:28.394
NARRATOR: Always compute the point and the
slope before writing the tangent line.

112
00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:33.349
NARRATOR: When solving a derivative problem
from first principles, use five checks.

113
00:07:33.349 --> 00:07:37.558
NARRATOR: First, identify the two inputs and
the interval change h.

114
00:07:37.558 --> 00:07:43.170
NARRATOR: Second, form the difference quotient
with parentheses around the entire numerator.

115
00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.550
NARRATOR: Third, simplify while h is nonzero.

116
00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:52.022
NARRATOR: Fourth, take the limit only after
the removable algebraic obstacle is handled.

117
00:07:52.022 --> 00:07:57.571
NARRATOR: Fifth, interpret the result with a
slope, rate, units, or tangent line.

118
00:07:57.571 --> 00:08:01.986
NARRATOR: Common errors include substituting
zero too early, dropping parentheses,

119
00:08:01.986 --> 00:08:08.609
confusing f of a with f prime of a, and
assuming every continuous point is

120
00:08:08.609 --> 00:08:09.050
differentiable.

121
00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:13.693
NARRATOR: The algebra matters, but the
interpretation is what tells you whether the

122
00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:15.836
answer belongs to the original problem.

123
00:08:15.962 --> 00:08:20.006
NARRATOR: The derivative begins with two
points and an average rate of change.

124
00:08:20.006 --> 00:08:23.998
NARRATOR: A limit brings those points together
without dividing by zero.

125
00:08:23.998 --> 00:08:29.499
NARRATOR: When the limit exists, it gives the
tangent slope and instantaneous rate at one

126
00:08:29.499 --> 00:08:29.866
input.

127
00:08:29.866 --> 00:08:34.457
NARRATOR: Repeating the process across inputs
creates the derivative function.

128
00:08:34.457 --> 00:08:39.397
NARRATOR: Its sign and value describe local
behavior, its failures mark important

129
00:08:39.397 --> 00:08:44.749
geometric features, its physical units
describe rates such as velocity, and its value

130
00:08:44.749 --> 00:08:48.042
supplies the slope of a local linear
approximation.

131
00:08:48.042 --> 00:08:50.516
NARRATOR: These are not unrelated definitions.

132
00:08:50.516 --> 00:08:53.692
NARRATOR: They are different views of the same
mathematical object.

133
00:08:53.692 --> 00:08:58.822
NARRATOR: If you can move fluently among the
quotient, the graph, the units, and the

134
00:08:58.822 --> 00:09:03.610
application, you understand the derivative at
a foundation strong enough for the rules and

135
00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:04.636
applications that follow.
